# Neon Postgres pooled connection string (Project > Connect > Pooled connection).
DATABASE_URL=

# Bearer token required by the admin-only write API (Phase 2+). Generate a
# long random value and keep it out of version control. Also gates the
# admin UI's `admin_session` cookie (proxy.ts / lib/admin-session.ts).
API_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# PBKDF2 salt for the admin-UI password login (app/admin/login/actions.ts).
# Required: login fails closed (every attempt rejected) if unset.
API_ADMIN_TOKEN_SALT=

# Salt for hashing submitter IPs used for public-contribution rate limiting.
# REQUIRED in production — hashIp() throws on Vercel production if unset,
# rather than silently hashing with the repo-public default salt. Falls back
# to a built-in default in dev, tests, and preview deployments (previews do
# not inherit Production-scoped env vars).
#
# SECOND CONSUMER since 2026-09-27: `lib/subscribe-consent.ts` derives the
# subscribe-consent cookie's HMAC key from this salt, under its own KEY_DOMAIN
# so the two uses cannot collide. Chosen over a new variable because a new var
# is invisible to already-built deployments and the feature would have failed
# closed until a redeploy. Consequence for rotation: changing this also
# invalidates every outstanding consent cookie — harmless (30-minute lifetime;
# affected users just get the ordinary double-opt-in email) but it is no longer
# true that only IP hashes depend on this value. See docs/maintainers.md.
CONTRIBUTE_IP_SALT=

# Email alerts (Resend) — all optional. If RESEND_API_KEY is unset, subscription
# confirm/alert sends become a logged no-op, so builds, tests, and CI run fine
# without it (lib/email.ts).

# Resend API key for sending the double-opt-in confirm + change-alert emails.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# "From" address for alert emails. Falls back to
# "Compute Atlas <alerts@compute-atlas.com>" if unset.
EMAIL_FROM=

# Recipient for contact-form notification emails (lib/email.ts). If unset,
# the message is still stored — only the notification send is skipped
# (logged, not an error).
CONTACT_TO_EMAIL=

# Public base URL for links inside emails (confirm/unsubscribe). Falls back to
# the built-in siteConfig.url if unset.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# Monthly state digest (app/api/cron/state-digest) — DISABLED BY DEFAULT, and
# deliberately so. Leave BOTH of these unset unless you are turning the digest
# on on purpose: the `state` subscription rows predate the feature and have
# never received mail, so enabling it resumes mail to a long-silent list.
# Read the route file's header first — it carries the enable checklist,
# including the idempotency prerequisite that must be closed BEFORE the switch
# is flipped (nothing here is idempotent yet; a repeat run re-sends).

# Bearer secret Vercel Cron presents as `Authorization: Bearer <value>`. Unset
# means every cron-path caller is rejected (fail-closed). MUST NOT equal
# API_ADMIN_TOKEN — if they match, the admin `?since=&until=` recovery path
# becomes unreachable (the cron check short-circuits first).
CRON_SECRET=

# Kill switch for the digest. Only the exact string "true" enables it; unset or
# anything else returns 503 and sends nothing.
STATE_DIGEST_ENABLED=

# "Email me when reviewed" — a one-shot notify request a contributor can
# attach to a single submission on /contribute. Kill switch, same contract as
# STATE_DIGEST_ENABLED above: only the exact string "true" enables it. Unset
# means the intake ignores the `notifyEmail` field entirely (reads nothing,
# validates nothing, writes nothing) and the review path sends nothing — the
# feature is a genuine no-op, not merely hidden in the UI.
SUBMISSION_NOTIFY_ENABLED=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Added 2026-09-27 (security audit s169)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Shared secret proving a request actually came through Cloudflare. A Transform
# Rule injects it as the request header `x-edge-shared-secret`; `proxy.ts`
# compares it with timingSafeEqual over SHA-256 digests.
# ⚠️ That exact header name — Cloudflare reserves `cf-` and `x-cf-` prefixes, and
# a mismatch between the rule and the code is an outage once fail-closed.
#
# WHY: the Vercel origin answers on the production custom domain at its anycast
# IP, so `curl --resolve www.compute-atlas.com:443:<origin-ip>` reaches the app
# directly and skips the WAF, the rate-limit rule, the country block and the
# edge cache. Authorization is unaffected (all origin-side), but every bypassed
# byte bills Vercel, and the project sits at the 100 GB Hobby cap.
#
# ⚠️ FAILS OPEN while unset — deliberately. The Cloudflare Transform Rule is
# applied by hand, and a fail-closed default would take production down the
# moment this shipped. Wire it in this order: Transform Rule first, then
# `vercel env add`, then REDEPLOY (a new env var is invisible to an
# already-built deployment). Only then consider flipping to fail-closed; the
# comment in `proxy.ts` says exactly what to change.
# ⚠️ `/api/cron/*` is exempt unconditionally — Vercel Cron may reach the
# deployment without traversing Cloudflare. It has its own CRON_SECRET bearer.
# Checked only when VERCEL_ENV === "production", so previews are unaffected.
EDGE_SHARED_SECRET=

# Kill switch for the discovery pipeline's mechanical source-verification gate
# (local Ollama). Polarity is the OPPOSITE of the two switches above: the gate
# is ON by default and only the exact string "false" disables it — a typo
# ("flase", "0", "no", "") leaves verification ENABLED, which is the safe
# direction. Undocumented until the s169 audit, despite being the one switch
# that can let unverified candidates reach staging.
VERIFY_SOURCES_ENABLED=

# Per-preview escape hatch: point a preview deployment at a live Neon branch
# instead of the bundled JSON snapshot (see lib/db/client.ts). Not a security
# control; documented because an undocumented var invites guessing.
PREVIEW_USE_DB=

# Staging-only intake token: accepted by POST /api/submissions ALONE. Give this
# (not API_ADMIN_TOKEN) to the discovery pipeline — it cannot publish a live
# facility, approve a submission, or sign an admin session cookie. Must be an
# independent value from API_ADMIN_TOKEN, or there is no separation at all.
#
# If unset, the pipeline falls back to API_ADMIN_TOKEN and nothing changes —
# merging the split is a behavioural no-op until it is set. ⚠️ Setting it is NOT
# what realises the benefit: the pipeline keeps its admin token until you also
# REMOVE API_ADMIN_TOKEN from the pipeline's .env.local. See docs/maintainers.md.
API_INTAKE_TOKEN=
